On Nov 27, 6:41 am, Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com>
wrote:

> > Is there any real point to classifying and carrying
> > a bunch of gems around that early in the game?
>
> Throwing at unicorns to distinguish precious ones from
> glass, and then to increase your luck by throwing named
> (non-glass) gems at a co-aligned unicorn.

That would require being fast enough to manage to
get myself in a straight line with a unicorn.  I suppose
if I found early speed boots...  but usually this is very
much a mid-game option -- by which time I usually[1]
get lucky enough to find a co-aligned altar, making
sacrifests an option as well.  I suppose if my dungeon
did not contain a co-aligned altar, throwing gems to
unicorns could be a reasonable substitute for getting
luck up, but again, we're talking mid-game here.

> Then, if you need money,

Need money, in a dungeon that has the gross
national product plus the national debt laying
around on the ground in gold coins?  Piles of
double- and even triple-digit numbers of gold
coins are literally more common than rocks
(which, incidentally, gets very annoying when
I play as a Caveman).  Why on earth would
I "need money"?

I've been picking up a couple of thousand coins
in case I find a shop and leaving the rest until I
get a bag of holding or locate an aligned priest.
The last thing I need to do is overtax myself by
carrying around all that gold, let alone everything
that can be sold for money.

Later, when I have a blessed bag of holding and
dragon scale mail and a ring of slow digestion, then
yeah, sure, I can make half a dozen trips through
the dungeon and round it all up.

In the early game, that's not a reasonable option.

> from priests.

You have to find a priest first.  A couple of times I've
gotten lucky, but usually I don't find one until Minetown,
by which time my pet is often strong enough to kill
him.  I can prevent that if the temple is fully enclosed
with a lockable door, which so far is about a third of
my games.

> > [1] With no bag of holding and early-game
> >     strength, anything not immediately vital to
> >     survival must be left.  Haha, you want
> >     to wear armor AND carry both melee and
> >     projectile weapons, plus food?  You'll be
> >     burdened the first time you pick up a
> >     couple of potions or scrolls.  Forget tools,
> >     except maybe a key (or a unicorn horn, if
> >     you can get your hands on one).
>
> Actually I think this is bad advice. Tools are amongst
> the most useful items in Nethack; there's a reason why
> they are made heavy[*]. A stethoscope is a life saver.

If I find a stethoscope, I generally hang onto it if at all
possible, and the same goes for a whistle or, failing
that, a non-cursed leash.  The most useful tool (other
than a bag, of course) that is at ALL likely to be found
in the early game is the key, which weighs little, so
that's good.  A bag (of any kind other than tricks)
goes without saying, as obtaining one is a key goal
in the early game.  (I know containers are technically
classified as tools in Nethack, but I think of them as
being their own category.)

In the middle-game, or if I'm playing a high-strength
character (e.g., a Valkyrie), I like to carry a pick-axe,
but I usually can't afford to until I get a bag of holding,
sometimes not even then if I'm not very strong,
because of the weight.  (In Gehennom a pick-axe is
so valuable it's worth leaving a piece of armor behind,
but not in the early game when your AC is so poor
and your hitpoints so low that you're constantly in
danger of being deep-sixed by almost anything that
moves.  I've never yet been killed by a grid bug or
newt, IIRC, but almost every other kind of monster
has put me under the daisies at some point.)

> The mentioned camera as well.

I've read about its immense value on the wiki, but
I've thus far been unable to verify it.  Messing around
flashing an ineffectual camera at monsters instead
of shooting them with darts or running away has
gotten me killed several times, and I've never gotten
it to actually work to any meaningful extent.  Usually
it doesn't scare them, and even if it does they "flee"
(i.e., briefly stop attacking but continue to remain
adjacent and chase me if I step away") for 1-2 turns
and *then* kill me.  When it works at all, it buys just
about enough time to write "Elbc?etn" in the dust
with my fingers.  Meh.  Not worth being burdened.
Let me carry a dagger instead -- it works at longer
range and has a chance of killing weak enemies
outright or wounding more powerful ones before
they reach me so that once they step into melee
range I can kill them more quickly and maybe even
survive.

Speaking of Elbereth, I've also had a really hard
time getting that to work as advertised, NOT for lack
of trying, especially in the early game when you don't
have enough hitpoints (even if you start full) to survive
long enough to try enough times to get it written
successfully, and since you don't have regeneration
yet you can recover two or at most three hitpoints
before it degrades again, and if you try to engrave a
second Elbereth it messes up the first one and then
you most certainly WILL die.

The exception is engrave-testing wands (while
not under attack), which does work mostly as
advertised, which is cool.

> Horns and drums, even the non-magic ones, also.

Those aren't particularly common in the early game,
so I haven't experimented very much with them yet.

> A tinning kit

Ditto.  Also, it weighs more than almost any weapon
and almost as much as the lighter pieces of armor, so
it is difficult to imagine an early-game character from a
non-strong role lugging it around prior to obtaining a bag
of holding (or gauntlets of power).

With that said, yeah, if you can bear the weight, it is
an undeniably useful item.  I've found tinning kits so
far in exactly one game -- my current Barbarian game
on my home system.  Of course, this one game makes
up some territory by having had *three* tinning kits, so
needless to say my strength is now 18/**, which is nice.
But even in that game I didn't find the first tinning kit
until Gehennom.

> Lamps to increase survival chance in dark areas.

Agreed.

> Sacks and bags to protect your loot.

I usually just carry one, but yes, the first portable
container you find is invaluable.  (If I have a bag of
holding, I might also carry a plain sack for things I
don't want to put in the bag of holding.)

One of the most important advantages of the
Valkyrie role is that you can actually carry a
(much more common) large box in the early
game, until you can find a bag. Try *that* as
a Tourist, ha.

> Towels and blindfolds,

Again, though, I only carry one of these (unless
I have a bag of holding, then I might carry one spare).

> [*] Never understood, though, why orbs are so extremely
> heavy compared to other tools, and they have drawbacks
> as well; I leave those alone.

I've not experimented much with glass orbs yet, so
I haven't formed any major opinions about them.

